 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law and the public health law, in relation to\namending physician assistant practice standards",
    "basePrintNo": "S9038",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9038-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§6542 & 6810, Ed L; amd §3702, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9038\n\nSPONSOR: MAY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law and the public health law, in relation\nto amending physician assistant practice standards\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo authorize physician assistants to practice without the supervision of\na physician under certain circumstances.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends § 6542 of the education law by adding language to\ndescribe the circumstances when a physician assistant is authorized to\npractice without the supervision of a physician. The circumstances are:\nwhen the physician assistant has practiced for more than 8,000 hours and\nis practicing in primary care or is employed by a health system or\nhospital and the health system or hospital determines the physician\nassistant meets the qualification of the medical staff bylaws and gives\nthe physician assistant privileges; and where a physician assistant has\ncompleted a program approved by the department of health in consultation\nwith the commissioner of education, when such services are performed\nwithin the scope of such program and specifies that where supervision is\nrequired it shall be continuous but not be construed as necessarily\nrequiring the physical presence of the supervising physician at the time\nand place where such services are performed.\n\nSection one adds physician assistants to the professionals who may\nprescribe and order a patient specific order or non-patient specific\nregimen to a licensed pharmacist or registered professional nurse for\nspecific tasks and tests.\n\n§ 2 of the bill amends subdivision 1 of § 2701 of the public health law\nto say that regulations defining and restricting the duties of physician\nassistants should be consistent with § 6542 of the education law as\namended in section one.\n\n§ 3 of the bill amends subdivisions 1 and 3 of § 3702 of the public\nhealth law to add durable medical equipment to what a physician assist-\nant may order for inpatient medical orders and on prescriptions for\ncontrolled substances adds language reflecting new language in S 6542 of\nthe education law as amended in section one.\n\n§ 4 of the bill amends § 3703 of the public health law to add language\nmaking the section consistent with new language in § 6542 of the educa-\ntion law as amended in section one.\n\n§ 5 of the bill amends paragraph a of subdivision 2 of § 902 of the\neducation law to authorize a physician assistant to perform the duties\nof the director of school health services.\n\n§ 6 of the bill amends subdivision 5 of § 6810 of the education law by\nstriking \"doctor's\" and replacing with \"prescriber's\" name for\nprescription records.\n\n\n§ 7 of the bill amends subdivision 27 of § 3302 of the public health law\nto add physician assistants to the list of practitioners permitted to\ndispense, administer or conduct research with respect to a controlled\nsubstance.\n\n§ 8 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn the fifty years since New York State established the profession and\npractice of physician assistant, the enabling statutory language has had\nfew, if any, amendments while the health care delivery system has\nchanged considerably. Stagnation.in the statutory language has caused\nconfusion and created barriers to access to care. PAs must undergo a\nrigorous course of medical education and training and are authorized to\npractice in virtually any medical field doctors are trained in.\n\nThey develop competencies working with physicians and in a team-based\ncare environment. Unfortunately, language in the current statutory\nframework, like the word \"supervisory,\" is frequently misinterpreted to\nimpose limitations on, or erect barriers to, physician assistants' prac-\ntice.\n\nDuring the COVID-19 pandemic, the Governor explicitly waived any \"super-\nvisory\" requirements for physician assistants, recognizing that\nincreased flexibility to allow greater access to care was imperative\nduring the public health crisis. Multiple hospital systems and physician\npractices across the state have benefitted from the elimination of\nbarriers to care - not to mention all persons in New York State. This\nbill would make permanent some of the changes permitted during the\npandemic while recognizing the changes that have occurred over the past\nfifty years to the state's health care delivery system.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATION:\n\nSavings to be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n120 days after it shall have become a law.\n",
